data.content:
# EF Core 10 Breaking Changes for Your Code

## 1. `SetProperty` value argument must be a lambda expression

In EF Core 10, `SetProperty`'s value parameter changed from `TProperty` to `Expression<Func<TSource, TProperty>>`. Your `Expression.Constant("foo")` is no longer valid as a bare constant — it must be wrapped in a lambda:

```csharp
// Before (EF Core 9) — bare constant as value:
arguments: new Expression[]
{
    Expression.Lambda<Func<Blog, string>>(
        Expression.Property(blogParameter, nameof(Blog.Name)), blogParameter),
    Expression.Constant("foo")
}

// After (EF Core 10) — value wrapped in a lambda:
var sParameter = Expression.Parameter(typeof(Blog), "b2");
arguments: new Expression[]
{
    Expression.Lambda<Func<Blog, string>>(
        Expression.Property(blogParameter, nameof(Blog.Name)), blogParameter),
    Expression.Lambda<Func<Blog, string>>(
        Expression.Constant("foo"), sParameter)
}
```

## 2. Complex type column naming — ambiguous properties now require explicit mapping

EF Core 10 no longer silently disambiguates columns when multiple complex properties share property names. You must configure column names explicitly:

```csharp
modelBuilder.Entity<Customer>(b =>
{
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.ShippingAddress, ab =>
    {
        ab.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("ShippingAddress_Street");
    });
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.BillingAddress, ab =>
    {
        ab.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("BillingAddress_Street");
    });
});

// For nested: Order.Payment.BillingAddress.City
modelBuilder.Entity<Order>(b =>
{
    b.ComplexProperty(o => o.Payment, pb =>
    {
        pb.ComplexProperty(p => p.BillingAddress, ab =>
        {
            ab.Property(a => a.City).HasColumnName("Payment_BillingAddress_City");
        });
    });
});
```

## 3. `IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger` — new `CommandSource` parameter

EF Core 10 added a `CommandSource` parameter to the diagnostics interface methods:

```csharp
public class MyLogger : IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger
{
    public InterceptionResult<DbDataReader> CommandReaderExecuting(
        IRelationalConnection connection,
        DbCommand command,
        DbContext context,
        Guid commandId,
        Guid connectionId,
        DateTimeOffset startTime,
        CommandSource commandSource)   // ← new parameter
    { /* logging */ }
}
```

`CommandSource` is an enum indicating where the command originated (`LinqQuery`, `ExecuteUpdate`, `Migrations`, `SaveChanges`, etc.), useful for filtering diagnostics.

---

**Summary:** The expression-tree change is the subtlest — existing code compiles but throws at runtime because EF Core 10's translator expects a `LambdaExpression` node for the value argument. The other two are compile-time breaks that surface immediately.
